# Private-name patterns - one regex per line. Lines starting with # are comments.
#
# What this file is for: names that must never enter your files. A client under
# NDA, or someone you keep off the record. The save checks (the pre-commit and
# commit-msg hooks) scan what you save and refuse the save if one of these names
# is in it.
#
# Empty is fine, and it is how this file starts. While it is empty the name
# check is off. The secret and attribution checks run anyway, with or
# without anything in this file.
#
# Never add your own name. Your identity file is tracked once you own your
# history, and your own name as a pattern would block every save that touches it.
#
# This file (scripts/private-name-patterns.txt) is gitignored, so the names in it
# never enter your history or any push. Only the .template copy ships.
#
# FORMAT - one Python regex per line:
#   \bClientUnderNDA\b             a single name, word-boundary matched
#   \bA\s+Person\s+Off\s+Record\b  a full name (use \s+ between words)
#   \bname\.at\.client\.com\b      an address
#
# TIP: block a distinctive name on its own, but a common first name only as a
# full name. A lone \bSam\b or \bLee\b will refuse ordinary saves.
#
# To turn the name check on, add lines below this one without the leading #.
